  "requestTimestamp": "2026-07-19T06:09:52.225Z",
  "answer": "**Filing-deadline note:** TINA cannot confirm a date-relative filing claim (e.g. \"today is the last day\") without your exact taxable year, return type, and the operative BIR deadline for that period. The general annual individual income-tax return deadline is on or before April 15 of the following year; a different date would apply only under a specific BIR extension, weekend/holiday adjustment, or special rule. Please confirm the taxable period and return type.\n\n### Short Answer\nYes, today is the last day to file the annual income tax return of an individual, which is due on or before the fifteenth (15th) day of April each year.\n\n### Controlling Authorities\nThis requirement is established under Section 51(C)(1) of the National Internal Revenue Code (NIRC), which states that the return of any individual shall be filed on or before April 15 of each year covering income for the preceding taxable year.\n\n### Interpretation\nThe NIRC mandates that individual taxpayers must submit their income tax returns by April 15. This deadline applies to all individuals, including citizens and resident aliens, who are required to report their income for the previous year.\n\n### Practical Meaning\nTaxpayers must ensure that their income tax returns are filed by today to avoid penalties and interest for late filing. It is advisable for individuals to verify that all necessary documents are prepared and submitted to the appropriate BIR office or authorized agent bank by the deadline.",
